# Implement impact-scoped recomputation

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical backend evidence is partial. The finite contracts below are
explicitly theoretical wherever normalized protocol or producer work lacks.

Compute changed semantic identities and dependency-expanded invalidation so a
small command does not regenerate unrelated notebook content, while every
derived layout, handwriting, diagnostic, preview, export, or motion result that
can change is recomputed.

Current executable direct-edit simulation separates semantic changes from
conservative derived-impact seeds for text, structured content, and page-profile
edits. Those seeds identify safe starting scopes and authority families, but the
frozen command contract explicitly treats them as inputs to later expansion.

No executable dependency graph currently relates those seeds to downstream flow
regions or layout, handwriting, diagnostic, preview, export, and motion
producers. Full impact expansion and incremental recomputation are blocked on
that derived-dependency authority; seeds must not be relabeled as the final
impact set.

## Finite changed-region and derived-dependency closure contract

**Inputs:** one accepted semantic transaction receipt, exact base/new
revisions, a declared dependency graph of semantic objects and derived
producers, and caches keyed to accepted authority. **Outputs:** a set of
directly changed semantic IDs, a dependency-expanded invalidation set and
read-only unaffected cache keys, or `UnknownDependencies` requiring safe
full invalidation. A direct-edit impact seed is not a complete final set.

**First-release graph:** define nodes for source text, flow/layout boxes,
handwriting stroke plan, diagnostics, preview render, Export/PDF result and
Live motion projection. Every derived result carries the upstream semantic
revision and exact profile/material/output options. Edges are explicit,
versioned and acyclic; page overflow and line-break changes can propagate
to later siblings on the same flow chain. On a missing edge or unknown
upstream owner, invalidate the complete relevant producer rather than reuse
an apparently unrelated stale page.

**Worked example:** notebook revision `r30` has two independent fixed pages.
On `p1`, text span `t1` precedes flow sibling `t2`; on fixed `p2`, figure
`f9` has no dependency from `p1`. Edit `t1` from `A` to `Longer answer`,
committing `r31`. The direct impact is `t1`.

The closure invalidates the
p1 flow from `t1` through `t2`, their handwriting layouts, overflow
screens, p1 Preview, whole-document PDF result and any Live plan consuming
p1. The `f9` source shape/own geometry remains unaffected if its profile
and page-scoped authority truly do not depend on `p1`. A whole-document
artifact may still require regeneration even if one component remains valid.

**Refusal example:** the graph has an unmodeled cross-page reference from
`t1` to `f9`, a cycle in derived producer dependencies or a cache entry
with revision `r30` but no owner. Do not emit the small closure as complete.
Return `UnknownDependencies` and invalidate the conservative full dependent
pipeline, or refuse incremental execution until the graph is repaired.
This safe failure cannot silently keep old PDF/motion bytes after accepted
source changes.

**Authority limits:** deterministic stamps identify one derived result under
accepted semantic/profile/paper/material choices and output mode. A cache
entry is reusable only if all those inputs and dependency identities remain
exact. Recompute invalidated nodes in topological order, publish results only
when the input accepted revision has not changed, and treat cancellation as
stale derived work rather than semantic rollback.

**Assumptions and limits:** typed edit-impact seeds and conservative scope
classification already exist. Runtime dependency graph, diff-to-flow
propagation, cache stamp invalidation, incremental scheduling and derived
producer integration do not. The example is a theoretical graph contract,
not proof that the current Preview, PDF or motion plan recomputes correctly.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for conservative semantic impact seeds; theoretical
  for dependency-expanded recomputation, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** one direct edit, downstream closure across seven producer
  classes, unaffected independent figure and unknown-edge refusal.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** executable dependency graph,
  incremental worker, cache invalidation or full output recomputation.
